20 20 190</pre></details><label>Import KT3D.PAR settings<input type="file" id="par" accept=".par,.txt"></label><p id="parInfo" class="small">PAR file paths are informational: select local data files yourself. Import maps fields by their original column positions.</p></section><section><h2>2 · Kriging and variogram model</h2><div class="grid two"><label>Kriging method<select id="type"><option value="1">Ordinary kriging · unknown mean</option><option value="0">Simple kriging · known mean</option><option value="2">Nonstationary SK · KT3D type 2</option><option value="3">External-drift kriging</option></select></label><label>Known mean · simple kriging<input id="mean" type="number" value="180" step="any"></label><label>Nugget variance<input id="nugget" type="number" value="50" step="any"></label></div><p class="small">Enter a fitted model, not experimental γ(h) values. Partial sills and nugget have squared variable units. Azimuth: 0° north–south, 90° east–west; GSLIB dip is positive downward; third angle rotates about the major axis.</p><div class="scroll model"><table id="structures"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Partial sill</th><th>Major range</th><th>Minor range</th><th>Vertical range</th><th>Azimuth °</th><th>Dip °</th><th>Third angle °</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><button id="addModel" class="secondary">Add structure</button><p id="sillInfo" class="small"></p><details><summary>Model conventions and drift options</summary><p class="small">Spherical reaches its sill at the entered range. Exponential uses exp(−3h/a); Gaussian uses exp(−9(h/a)²), exactly as in the supplied COVA3. For Gaussian, the entered a is three times the scale in exp(−(h/scale)²), or √3 times the range parameter in exp(−3(h/range)²). Hole effect uses cos(πh/a); it may not give a valid covariance for arbitrary 2D/3D configurations. Power uses the major-range entry as exponent (0 &lt; exponent ≤ 2), and minor/exponent and vertical/exponent as anisotropy ratios; its partial sill is a scale coefficient, with this COVA3’s total-covariance offset for each power term (999 contributes to the total per power structure). For introductory field work, start with a fitted spherical, exponential, or Gaussian model.</p><div id="drifts" class="checkrow"></div><label><input id="trend" type="checkbox">Estimate trend only · KT3D itrend = 1</label><p class="small">Polynomial drift adds the selected constraints to ordinary or external-drift kriging. Type 2 reproduces supplied KT3D behavior: the target secondary value is the local SK mean subtracted from all neighbor values. It does not subtract individual neighbor means. External drift requires a secondary value at every sample and target.</p></details></section><section><h2>3 · Search neighborhood</h2><div class="grid"><label>Major search radius<input id="r1" type="number" value="150" step="any"></label><label>Minor search radius<input id="r2" type="number" value="150" step="any"></label><label>Vertical search radius<input id="r3" type="number" value="150" step="any"></label><label>Search azimuth °<input id="az" type="number" value="0" step="any"></label><label>Search dip °<input id="dip" type="number" value="0" step="any"></label><label>Search third angle °<input id="roll" type="number" value="0" step="any"></label><label>Minimum neighbors<input id="min" type="number" value="4" step="any"></label><label>Maximum neighbors<input id="max" type="number" value="24" step="any"></label><label>Maximum per octant · 0 = off<input id="octant" type="number" value="0" step="any"></label></div><p class="small">Nearest samples are ranked by anisotropic search distance. Search axes are independent of variogram ranges. Octants are defined in unrotated X/Y/Z coordinates.</p></section><section><h2>4 · Estimation locations and support</h2><label>Target locations<select id="mode"><option value="grid">Regular grid</option><option value="nodes">Uploaded target nodes / jackknife</option><option value="cv">Leave-location-out cross-validation</option></select></label><div id="gridPanel"><div class="grid"><label>Number of X nodes<input id="xn" type="number" value="21" step="any"></label><label>First X center<input id="x0" type="number" value="0" step="any"></label><label>X spacing / block width<input id="xs" type="number" value="5" step="any"></label><label>Number of Y nodes<input id="yn" type="number" value="21" step="any"></label><label>First Y center<input id="y0" type="number" value="0" step="any"></label><label>Y spacing / block width<input id="ys" type="number" value="5" step="any"></label><label>Number of Z nodes<input id="zn" type="number" value="1" step="any"></label><label>First Z center<input id="z0" type="number" value="0" step="any"></label><label>Z spacing / block width<input id="zs" type="number" value="1" step="any"></label></div><button id="fit" class="secondary">Fit XY grid to sample bounds</button><p class="small">Grid origin is the first cell center. X varies fastest, then Y, then Z. The grid covers its rectangle; no field-boundary clipping is applied.</p></div><div id="nodesPanel" class="hidden"><label>Target nodes · CSV or GEO-EAS in selected format<input id="nodesFile" type="file" accept=".csv,.txt,.dat,.out"></label><div class="grid"><label>Target X<select id="nxf"><option value="">None</option></select></label><label>Target Y<select id="nyf"><option value="">None</option></select></label><label>Target Z · optional<select id="nzf"><option value="">None</option></select></label><label>Observed value · optional<select id="nvf"><option value="">None</option></select></label><label>Target secondary · optional<select id="nef"><option value="">None</option></select></label></div><p id="nodesInfo" class="small">Uploaded targets exclude colocated samples, matching KT3D jackknife mode. They can also carry independent observed values for validation.</p></div><div id="externalPanel"><label>Grid secondary values · CSV with header<input id="externalFile" type="file" accept=".csv"></label><label>Grid secondary value column<select id="externalField"><option value="">Choose field</option></select></label><p class="small">For type 2 or 3 on a regular grid: one row per node, in X-fastest grid order. For a block, this value represents the block’s secondary mean. No coordinate matching is performed.</p></div><h3>Block discretization</h3><div class="grid"><label>X discretization points<input id="dx" type="number" value="1" step="any"></label><label>Y discretization points<input id="dy" type="number" value="1" step="any"></label><label>Z discretization points<input id="dz" type="number" value="1" step="any"></label></div><p class="small">1 × 1 × 1 gives point kriging. More points approximate a block average over the entered grid cell dimensions; at most 64 total. Uploaded targets use those same cell dimensions. Validation requires point support.</p><button id="run">Calculate kriging</button><button id="cancel" class="secondary" disabled>Cancel</button><progress id="progress" value="0" max="1"></progress><div id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Load observations or try the teaching example.</div></section></div><aside><section><h2>Estimates and uncertainty</h2><div class="grid two"><label>Map color<select id="display"><option value="estimate">Kriging estimate</option><option value="sd">Kriging standard deviation</option><option value="variance">Kriging variance</option><option value="error">Validation error · estimate − observed</option><option value="count">Neighbor count</option></select></label><label>Z slice<select id="slice"><option>Run a calculation</option></select></label></div><div class="checkrow"><label><input id="samples" type="checkbox" checked>Show sample locations</label></div><canvas id="map" width="900" height="680" aria-label="Map of kriging results; numeric values are available in the table and downloadable CSV"></canvas><div class="legend"></div><div class="ends"><span id="low">—</span><span id="high">—</span></div><p id="hover">Hover over the map to inspect the nearest node.</p><div id="stats" class="stats"></div><p class="note small">Kriging uncertainty is conditional on the selected model, sample locations, and support. It does not measure all sources of error. A smooth map alone does not establish fertility management zones.</p><button id="csv" disabled>Results CSV</button><button id="geo" disabled>GEO-EAS results</button><button id="settings" disabled>Settings JSON</button><button id="png" class="secondary" disabled>Map PNG</button></section><section><h2>Results</h2><div id="table" class="scroll">Run kriging to populate the results table.</div><p class="small">Table previews the first 300 nodes; exports contain every node, including unestimated locations and their status. Import CSV in QGIS using X/Y and the original CRS. Outputs are points; block estimates represent averages centered at those points.</p></section><section><h2>Using this with the field variogram</h2><ol class="small"><li>Analyze the same variable and coordinate units in the variogram page.</li><li>Choose a plausible model with nugget, partial sill, range, and any supported anisotropy. This page does not fit models automatically.</li><li>Run cross-validation and examine bias, RMSE, and standardized errors before gridding.</li><li>Compare reasonable neighborhood and model settings. Retain only supported estimates within the field boundary for your analysis.</li></ol><p class="small">This is a JavaScript adaptation of the supplied continuous-variable KT3D routine and its GSLIB library sources, not a compiled Fortran executable. It uses double precision and a KD-tree search. Indicator-distribution mode and original debug files are not implemented. Singular systems are reported without adding an automatic nugget or averaging duplicate samples.</p></section></aside></main><footer>Field Kriging 1.1 · Based on supplied KT3D.F / KT3D.INC, version 2.000 · Original GSLIB copyright (C) 1996 Stanford University. Original 